  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "Can I reimburse myself out of my HSA?",
      "answer": "Yes, you can reimburse yourself out of your HSA for qualified medical expenses incurred after the account was established under state law. You must maintain records showing the expenses were not reimbursed by insurance or other sources and were not claimed as an itemized deduction on your tax return."
    },
    {
      "question": "Does HSA reimbursement require a receipt?",
      "answer": "Yes, IRS rules require you to keep receipts and documentation showing your distributions were used exclusively to pay or reimburse qualified medical expenses. You do not submit these receipts with your tax return, but you must retain them in your tax records to substantiate distributions reported on Form 8889."
    },
    {
      "question": "Can an FSA reimburse an expense before the service happens?",
      "answer": "No, an FSA cannot advance reimburse future or projected expenses. Under IRS Publication 969, expenses are only eligible for FSA reimbursement after the medical care has actually been provided during your active coverage period."
    },
    {
      "question": "Can I reimburse a medical membership fee?",
      "answer": "A medical membership fee can only be reimbursed if it meets specific federal tax criteria. Under IRS Notice 2026-5, an HSA can reimburse periodic fees for a qualifying direct primary care arrangement that provides solely primary care services from qualifying practitioners without general anesthesia, non-vaccine drugs, or complex laboratory tests. Fees for general health improvement or arrangements paid by employer contributions do not qualify."
    }
  ],
